@@ -437,9 +437,66 @@ Telegram User Account
 
 Never assume `bot_user_id == telegram_account_id`.
 
-Authentication may use: phone, login code, 2FA, QR authentication.
-
-Never store login codes or 2FA passwords. Telegram sessions are sensitive credentials.
+### 12.1 Authentication method: QR login ONLY — phone+code via bot chat is FORBIDDEN
+
+This service MUST authenticate Telegram user accounts using **Telethon's QR
+login flow** (`client.qr_login()`), and MUST NOT implement a phone-number +
+SMS/app login-code flow where the code is typed into the bot chat.
+
+**Why this is a hard rule, not a preference:** Telegram's servers detect the
+pattern "a login code was entered correctly, but from a different
+session/device than the one the sign-in is completing on" and treat it as
+your account credentials being phished — because that is exactly the shape
+of a real account-takeover attack (attacker asks victim to relay the code).
+Telegram blocks the login with a message like *"this code was previously
+shared"*, regardless of whether the code was correct or whether the parties
+involved consented to it. This is not something that can be fixed with a
+different retry strategy, disclaimers, or a consent checkbox in a privacy
+policy — it is server-side abuse detection independent of any agreement
+between the bot operator and the bot's user.
+
+QR login has no equivalent problem: the user scans the QR code using their
+own official Telegram client (the same client they're already logged into),
+which is the exact mechanism Telegram provides for linking a new
+device/session — the code/token is never typed into any chat.
+
+The original reference project (`Saveit.py`) avoids this problem entirely by
+being a single long-running script that the account owner runs themselves,
+entering the code directly into their own local process — not into a
+third-party bot's chat. That option is not available to a multi-tenant bot,
+which is why QR login is the only compliant path here.
+
+### 12.2 Each Telegram account authenticates with its OWN api_id/api_hash
+
+Do NOT use one global `TELEGRAM_API_ID`/`TELEGRAM_API_HASH` (from `.env`) to
+authenticate every connected account. Each `TelegramAccount` row MUST store
+its own `api_id` and encrypted `api_hash`, supplied by the account's owner
+from https://my.telegram.org, and the Telethon client for that account must
+be constructed using that account's own credentials — never the operator's
+global one.
+
+This mirrors how `Saveit.py` works (each person running the script gets
+their own `api_id`/`api_hash` from their own my.telegram.org app) and gives
+each user account isolated application-level identity on Telegram's side,
+independent of every other tenant.
+
+`api_hash` must be encrypted at rest with the same `SessionCipher` used for
+session strings (CLAUDE.md §13) — treat it as equally sensitive.
+
+### 12.3 One Telegram account per application user (no multi-account per customer)
+
+Each application user (the person who `/start`s the bot) may connect **at
+most one** Telegram account. Do not allow a single user to add multiple
+Telegram accounts. Enforce this at the service layer (reject
+`create_account` if the user already has a non-disconnected account) — not
+just in the bot's UI copy.
+
+If a user needs to switch accounts, they must `/disconnect` the existing one
+first.
+
+Never store login codes or 2FA passwords beyond the single request needed to
+use them immediately. Telegram sessions (and now, per-account api_hash) are
+sensitive credentials — see CLAUDE.md §13.
 
 ---
 
